<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 1: SOC OVERVIEW ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec1" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-building-shield text-emerald-400"></i> 1. SOC Overview</h2>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🎯 What is a SOC?</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">A Security Operations Center (SOC) is a centralized function within an organization employing people, processes, and technology to continuously monitor and improve an organization's security posture while preventing, detecting, analyzing, and responding to cybersecurity incidents. A SOC acts as the central command point for cybersecurity operations, where analysts watch dashboards, investigate alerts, and coordinate responses around the clock — typically operating on a 24x7x365 basis since attacks do not respect business hours.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-6">
    <div class="section-card p-5">
      <div class="badge badge-blue mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-building"></i> Internal SOC</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Fully owned and staffed by the organization itself. Provides maximum control over data, tooling, and culture, but requires significant investment in hiring, training, and 24x7 staffing rotations. Common in large enterprises, banks, and government bodies with strict data residency requirements.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-card p-5">
      <div class="badge badge-amber mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-people-arrows"></i> Virtual / Co-Managed SOC</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A hybrid model where the organization's internal team partners with an external provider. The internal team typically retains Tier 2/3 responsibilities and context, while the external partner supplements with Tier 1 monitoring, tooling, or after-hours coverage. Balances cost with retained institutional knowledge.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-card p-5">
      <div class="badge badge-red mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-cloud"></i> Outsourced / MSSP</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A Managed Security Service Provider runs the entire SOC function on behalf of the client. Offers fast time-to-value and lower upfront costs, ideal for small/medium businesses without security expertise. Trade-offs include less visibility into internal context and reliance on the provider's SLAs for response times.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📌 The "Need" for a SOC</h3>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-2">
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-briefcase text-blue-400 mt-1"></i>
        <div>
          <div class="font-semibold text-slate-200 text-sm mb-1">Business Justification</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Protects brand reputation, ensures regulatory compliance (GDPR, HIPAA, PCI-DSS, ISO 27001), reduces financial losses from breaches, and maintains customer trust. A documented SOC function is often a contractual requirement for enterprise clients and cyber insurance providers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-microchip text-emerald-400 mt-1"></i>
        <div>
          <div class="font-semibold text-slate-200 text-sm mb-1">Technical Justification</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Provides centralized visibility across a sprawling attack surface (endpoints, cloud, network, identity). Enables faster detection and response (reducing dwell time), correlates signals that would be invisible in isolated silos, and creates a feedback loop for continuous hardening.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">👥 SOC Roles &amp; Responsibilities</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="soc-table">
      <tbody><tr><th>Role</th><th>Primary Responsibility</th><th>Typical Activities</th></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-blue">Tier 1</span> Analyst (Triage)</td><td>First line of defense — alert monitoring and initial triage</td><td>Monitors SIEM dashboards, performs initial alert validation, escalates true positives, closes false positives with documentation, follows runbooks for common alert types.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-amber">Tier 2</span> Analyst (Incident Responder)</td><td>Deep investigation and active incident handling</td><td>Performs in-depth log analysis, correlates multiple data sources, executes containment actions (isolating hosts, blocking IOCs), coordinates with IT for remediation, documents incident timelines.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-red">Tier 3</span> Analyst (Threat Hunter)</td><td>Proactive threat hunting and advanced analysis</td><td>Conducts hypothesis-driven hunts for undetected threats, performs malware reverse engineering, develops and tunes detection rules, leads root-cause analysis for major incidents, mentors junior analysts.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-green">SOC Manager</span></td><td>Operational leadership and strategic oversight</td><td>Manages team scheduling and performance, reports metrics (MTTD/MTTR) to leadership, owns vendor relationships and tooling roadmap, ensures compliance with frameworks and SLAs, handles escalations to executive stakeholders.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🛡️ Blue Teaming Philosophy</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">Blue Teaming refers to the defensive side of cybersecurity operations — the practice of protecting an organization's assets through continuous monitoring, detection, and hardening. Unlike a Red Team, which simulates attacks to find weaknesses, the Blue Team's core philosophy centers on three pillars: <strong class="text-emerald-400">defensive security</strong> (implementing controls such as firewalls, EDR, and access policies to prevent compromise), <strong class="text-emerald-400">continuous hardening</strong> (proactively reducing attack surface through patching, configuration baselines, and least-privilege enforcement), and <strong class="text-emerald-400">continuous monitoring</strong> (maintaining persistent visibility via SIEM, EDR telemetry, and log analysis to detect anomalies in real time). The Blue Team mindset assumes breach is inevitable and focuses on minimizing dwell time and impact when it occurs.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 2: SIEM ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec2" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-server text-blue-400"></i> 2. SIEM — Security Information and Event Management</h2>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🎯 Core Concepts</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-3">A SIEM platform is the central nervous system of a SOC. It performs three foundational functions:</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-blue w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-database"></i> Log Aggregation</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Collects logs and events from disparate sources — firewalls, endpoints, servers, cloud platforms, applications — into a single centralized repository, eliminating the need for analysts to manually check dozens of separate systems.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-amber w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrows-to-dot"></i> Normalization</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Converts logs from varying formats and vendors into a standardized schema (e.g. Common Event Format), so a "failed login" from a Windows server and a Linux server can be correlated and searched identically.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-green w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-chart-line"></i> Analytics</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Applies correlation rules, statistical baselines, and machine learning to normalized data to surface anomalies — turning millions of raw events into a manageable number of actionable alerts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🛠️ Industry SIEM Tools — Comparative Analysis</h3>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mt-2">
      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-cloud-bolt text-purple-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">SentinelOne</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-3">Singularity Cloud / Core</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">SentinelOne's Singularity platform unifies EDR/XDR telemetry with SIEM-like data lake capabilities (Singularity Data Lake). It emphasizes AI-driven autonomous response — the agent can automatically kill processes and roll back ransomware changes without human intervention. Singularity Core focuses on endpoint protection, while Cloud extends coverage to cloud workloads, containers, and Kubernetes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-magnifying-glass text-orange-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">Splunk</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-3">Enterprise Security / SPL</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Splunk Enterprise Security (ES) is built atop Splunk's core indexing engine and uses the Search Processing Language (SPL) — a powerful pipe-based query language for searching, filtering, and visualizing massive datasets. SPL allows analysts to write complex correlation searches (e.g. <code class="text-emerald-400">index=auth action=failure | stats count by user</code>). Renowned for flexibility and a vast app ecosystem (Splunkbase), though licensing costs scale with data ingestion volume.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-network-wired text-blue-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">QRadar</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-green mb-3">IBM Correlation Engine</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">IBM QRadar is known for its robust built-in correlation engine that automatically builds an asset model and applies rules across log sources and network flow data (NetFlow/QFlow) simultaneously. Its Offense Magnitude scoring helps analysts prioritize the most severe correlated incidents first. QRadar's network flow analysis capability — inspecting raw traffic even without traditional logs — is a key differentiator from log-only SIEMs.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 3: SOAR ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec3" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-robot text-amber-400"></i> 3. SOAR — Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response</h2>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🎯 Core Concepts</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-3">SOAR platforms integrate disparate security tools (SIEM, EDR, firewalls, ticketing systems, threat intel feeds) into unified, automated workflows. The core value proposition is reducing <strong class="text-amber-400">Mean Time to Respond (MTTR)</strong> — the average time between an alert firing and the threat being contained.</p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">Automation is critical because Tier 1 analysts are often overwhelmed by repetitive, low-complexity tasks: enriching an IP address with threat intel, checking a hash against VirusTotal, or disabling a compromised user account. SOAR offloads these mechanical steps to automated playbooks, freeing analysts to focus on judgment-intensive decisions — and crucially, executing containment actions in seconds rather than the minutes or hours a human would take, which can be the difference between a contained incident and a full ransomware deployment.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📋 Playbooks</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-4">A playbook is a rule-based, automated workflow that defines exactly what steps should occur when a specific trigger condition is met. Playbooks typically follow an if-this-then-that logic chain and can include both fully automated actions and human-approval checkpoints ("human-in-the-loop").</p>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center gap-3">
      <div class="flow-box flex-1 w-full"><i class="fa-solid fa-bell text-red-400 mr-2"></i>Alert Triggered<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Phishing email reported</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow rotate-90 sm:rotate-0"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right"></i></div>
      <div class="flow-box flex-1 w-full"><i class="fa-solid fa-magnifying-glass text-blue-400 mr-2"></i>Auto-Enrichment<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Check sender, URL, attachment hash vs. threat intel</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow rotate-90 sm:rotate-0"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right"></i></div>
      <div class="flow-box flex-1 w-full"><i class="fa-solid fa-ban text-amber-400 mr-2"></i>Containment Action<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Quarantine email, block sender domain</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow rotate-90 sm:rotate-0"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right"></i></div>
      <div class="flow-box flex-1 w-full"><i class="fa-solid fa-ticket text-emerald-400 mr-2"></i>Ticket + Notify<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Case opened, analyst notified for review</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🛠️ Industry Examples</h3>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-2">
      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-bolt text-orange-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">Splunk SOAR</span></div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Formerly Phantom, Splunk SOAR provides a visual drag-and-drop playbook editor with Python code blocks for custom logic. It integrates tightly with Splunk ES for case management and has a large library of pre-built "apps" connecting to hundreds of third-party security tools.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-shield-cat text-yellow-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">Palo Alto Cortex XSOAR</span></div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Cortex XSOAR combines SOAR, threat intel management (TIM), and case management in one platform. It is heavily community-driven via the Cortex Marketplace, offering thousands of pre-built integrations and playbooks, and supports both automated and analyst-guided "war room" investigation workflows.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 4: EDR ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec4" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-laptop-medical text-emerald-400"></i> 4. EDR — Endpoint Detection and Response</h2>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🎯 Core Concepts</h3>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mt-2">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-blue w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-satellite-dish"></i> Endpoint Telemetry</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Continuous, granular data collection from endpoints — process creation, file modifications, registry changes, network connections, and user logon events. This raw telemetry is the foundational dataset upon which all EDR detection is built.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-amber w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-brain"></i> Behavioral Analysis</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Rather than relying solely on known-malware signatures, EDR builds a behavioral model of "normal" — flagging suspicious sequences like Microsoft Word spawning PowerShell, which spawns an encoded command, which makes an outbound connection.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-red w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-pause"></i> Host Isolation</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">When a host is confirmed compromised, EDR agents can remotely isolate it from the network (allowing only EDR management traffic) — containing lateral movement while analysts investigate, without physically unplugging the machine.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🛠️ Tool Breakdowns</h3>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mt-2">
      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-shield-dog text-cyan-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">Wazuh</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-green mb-3">Open Source</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A free, open-source security platform combining EDR-like host-based intrusion detection (HIDS), log analysis, file integrity monitoring (FIM), and vulnerability detection. Built on the Elastic Stack, Wazuh is popular in academic and budget-constrained SOC environments. Its agent-based architecture deploys lightweight agents that forward telemetry to a central manager for rule-based analysis.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-feather-pointed text-red-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">CrowdStrike Falcon</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-3">Cloud-Native</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A cloud-native EDR/XDR platform built around a single lightweight agent ("the Falcon sensor") that streams telemetry to CrowdStrike's cloud for analysis using AI/ML models and the Threat Graph. Known for minimal performance impact, rapid deployment, and industry-leading threat intelligence integration via the CrowdStrike Intelligence team.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-brands fa-windows text-blue-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">Microsoft Defender for Endpoint</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-3">Native Integration</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Deeply integrated into Windows itself, Defender for Endpoint requires no separate agent on Windows machines — leveraging the built-in Defender Antivirus and kernel-level sensors. Part of the Microsoft 365 Defender XDR suite, it provides attack surface reduction rules, automated investigation/remediation (AIR), and tight correlation with Entra ID (Azure AD) identity signals.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 5: XDR ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec5" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-diagram-project text-purple-400"></i> 5. XDR — Extended Detection and Response</h2>

  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🎯 The Evolution from EDR to XDR</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-4">XDR represents the natural evolution of EDR — extending the same detection-and-response philosophy beyond just the endpoint to encompass the entire IT environment. Where EDR provides deep visibility into a single host, XDR performs <strong class="text-purple-400">cross-layered data stitching</strong>, correlating telemetry from multiple security domains into a single unified timeline and detection model.</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-3 mt-2">
      <div class="flow-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-network-wired text-blue-400 mr-2"></i>Network<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Traffic flows, DNS, IDS/IPS alerts</span></div>
      <div class="flow-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-cloud text-cyan-400 mr-2"></i>Cloud<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">CASB, CSPM, workload logs</span></div>
      <div class="flow-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-id-badge text-amber-400 mr-2"></i>Identity<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Logon events, MFA, privilege changes</span></div>
      <div class="flow-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-desktop text-emerald-400 mr-2"></i>Endpoint<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Process trees, file activity</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mt-4">By stitching these layers together, XDR can detect multi-stage attacks that would appear as isolated, low-severity events in each individual tool — for example, an anomalous login from a new country (identity) followed minutes later by unusual data access on a file server (endpoint) and a large outbound transfer (network) is trivial to spot when correlated, but nearly invisible when each signal lives in its own silo. This reduces analyst fatigue from "alert sprawl" across multiple consoles and dramatically improves detection of advanced persistent threats (APTs).</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: ALERTS & TRIAGE ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec6" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-filter text-amber-400"></i> 6. Security Alerts &amp; Triage Funnel</h2>

  <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
    <div class="section-card p-6">
      <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🎯 Alert Triaging</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Triage is the process of separating noise (benign or low-priority alerts) from critical signals (genuine threats requiring action). Effective triage relies on context: asset criticality, alert severity, historical false-positive rate for that rule, and correlation with other recent events. A well-tuned SOC might receive tens of thousands of raw events daily but escalate only a handful of true incidents — the triage funnel is what makes this ratio manageable.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-card p-6">
      <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📡 Telemetry — Data Ingestion Pipelines</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Telemetry refers to the continuous stream of data collected from across the environment — logs, network flows, endpoint events, cloud audit trails — and the pipelines (often built on tools like Logstash, Fluentd, or vendor-native forwarders) that transport, parse, and normalize this data before it reaches the SIEM for analysis. Pipeline health directly determines detection quality: gaps in ingestion create blind spots.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📝 Alert Reporting — The 5 W's Format</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-4">Every alert investigation should be documented using the 5 W's to ensure consistency and completeness, regardless of which analyst picks it up next:</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-5 gap-3">
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/50"><div class="badge badge-blue mb-2">WHO</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Which user, host, or external actor is involved? Source and destination identities.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/50"><div class="badge badge-amber mb-2">WHAT</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">What action or behavior triggered the alert? What rule fired?</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/50"><div class="badge badge-green mb-2">WHEN</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Precise timestamp(s) of the activity — critical for building an attack timeline.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/50"><div class="badge badge-red mb-2">WHERE</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Which system, network segment, or geographic location did this occur on/from?</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/50"><div class="badge" style="background:rgba(168,85,247,.12);color:#c084fc;border:1px solid rgba(168,85,247,.3)">WHY</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">What is the likely intent or root cause? Is this part of a larger pattern?</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🔻 Visual: The Alert Triage Funnel</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-4">The funnel below illustrates how raw data is progressively filtered down to true incidents requiring response:</p>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
      <div class="w-full sm:w-[95%] flow-box bg-blue-500/10 border-blue-500/30"><i class="fa-solid fa-database text-blue-400 mr-2"></i>Data Streams<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Millions of raw logs/events per day — firewalls, endpoints, cloud, identity</span></div>
      <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-600"></i>
      <div class="w-full sm:w-[80%] flow-box bg-cyan-500/10 border-cyan-500/30"><i class="fa-solid fa-gears text-cyan-400 mr-2"></i>SIEM / Correlation Rules<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Normalization + rule matching + ML baselining reduces volume by orders of magnitude</span></div>
      <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-600"></i>
      <div class="w-full sm:w-[60%] flow-box bg-amber-500/10 border-amber-500/30"><i class="fa-solid fa-bell text-amber-400 mr-2"></i>Aggregated Alerts<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">Hundreds of alerts/day — Tier 1 triage and enrichment occurs here</span></div>
      <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-600"></i>
      <div class="w-full sm:w-[40%] flow-box bg-red-500/10 border-red-500/30"><i class="fa-solid fa-fire text-red-400 mr-2"></i>True Incidents<br><span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-normal">A handful per week — escalated to Tier 2/3 and IR process</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 7: INCIDENT CLASSIFICATION ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec7" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-sitemap text-blue-400"></i> 7. Incident Classification</h2>
  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📐 Event vs. Alert vs. Incident</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-4">A foundational distinction every SOC analyst must internalize is the difference between an event, an alert, and an incident — these terms are often used loosely outside the SOC but have precise meanings within it:</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-3"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-info"></i> EVENT</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-2">Any observable occurrence in a network or system. Events are the raw building blocks — a login, a file write, a DNS query, a process launch.</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 italic">Example: "User jdoe logged into WORKSTATION-04 at 09:14."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-3"><i class="fa-solid fa-bell"></i> ALERT</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-2">A notification triggered when a specific event (or pattern of events) matches a predefined detection rule. An alert says "something noteworthy happened" but does not confirm malicious intent.</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 italic">Example: "5 failed logins for jdoe within 60 seconds — possible brute force rule fired."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-red mb-3"><i class="fa-solid fa-fire"></i> INCIDENT</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-2">A confirmed security breach or violation of security policy — an alert that, after investigation, is determined to represent actual unauthorized activity requiring formal response per the IR process.</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 italic">Example: "Confirmed: jdoe's credentials were compromised via phishing and used for unauthorized VPN access."</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-5 flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
      <div class="flow-box flex-1"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-info text-blue-400 mr-2"></i>Event</div>
      <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right text-slate-600"></i>
      <div class="flow-box flex-1"><i class="fa-solid fa-filter text-amber-400 mr-2"></i>Matches Rule → Alert</div>
      <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right text-slate-600"></i>
      <div class="flow-box flex-1"><i class="fa-solid fa-magnifying-glass text-cyan-400 mr-2"></i>Validated by Analyst</div>
      <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right text-slate-600"></i>
      <div class="flow-box flex-1"><i class="fa-solid fa-fire text-red-400 mr-2"></i>Confirmed → Incident</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 8: INCIDENT RESPONSE ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec8" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-clipboard-list text-red-400"></i> 8. Incident Response (IR)</h2>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🔄 IR Lifecycle &amp; Steps</h3>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-3 mt-2">
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40"><div class="badge badge-blue mb-2">1. Preparation</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Establishing IR plans, tooling, training, communication channels, and runbooks before an incident occurs. The single most important phase — readiness determines response speed.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40"><div class="badge badge-amber mb-2">2. Detection &amp; Analysis</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Identifying that an incident has occurred via alerts, user reports, or threat hunting, then scoping its severity, affected systems, and root cause.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40"><div class="badge badge-red mb-2">3. Containment</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Limiting the spread and impact — isolating infected hosts, disabling compromised accounts, blocking malicious IPs/domains at the firewall.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40"><div class="badge badge-green mb-2">4. Eradication</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Removing the root cause entirely — deleting malware, closing the exploited vulnerability, removing attacker-created accounts or persistence mechanisms.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40"><div class="badge" style="background:rgba(168,85,247,.12);color:#c084fc;border:1px solid rgba(168,85,247,.3)">5. Recovery</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Restoring affected systems to normal operation, validating they are clean, and monitoring closely for signs of reinfection or attacker return.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40"><div class="badge badge-blue mb-2">6. Post-Incident Activity</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">The "Lessons Learned" phase — documenting the full timeline, identifying process gaps, and feeding improvements back into detection rules and preparation.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📚 Frameworks: NIST SP 800-61r2 vs. SANS IR</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="soc-table">
      <tbody><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>NIST SP 800-61 r2</th><th>SANS IR Framework</th></tr>
      <tr><td class="font-semibold text-slate-300">Structure</td><td>4 phases: Preparation; Detection &amp; Analysis; Containment, Eradication &amp; Recovery (combined); Post-Incident Activity</td><td>6 phases: Preparation; Identification; Containment; Eradication; Recovery; Lessons Learned (each phase distinct)</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="font-semibold text-slate-300">Origin &amp; Use</td><td>Published by the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology — often a compliance baseline for government and regulated industries.</td><td>Developed by the SANS Institute for practical, training-focused application — widely taught and used as a memorable operational checklist (PICERL).</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="font-semibold text-slate-300">Granularity</td><td>Treats containment, eradication, and recovery as a single iterative phase, reflecting that these often overlap in practice.</td><td>Separates each into a distinct phase, giving teams clearer checkpoints and handoff points during a live incident.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="font-semibold text-slate-300">Best Fit</td><td>Organizations needing audit-ready, standards-aligned documentation (federal agencies, critical infrastructure).</td><td>Teams prioritizing hands-on operational clarity and analyst training — common in commercial SOCs and certifications (GCIH).</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📄 Incident Reporting — Documentation Requirements</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-3">Thorough documentation is a legal, regulatory, and operational necessity. A complete incident report should include:</p>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-400">
      <li class="flex gap-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-400 mt-1"></i> Incident classification, severity rating, and unique case/ticket ID</li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-400 mt-1"></i> Full timeline of events with timestamps (using the 5 W's format from Section 6)</li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-400 mt-1"></i> Affected systems, accounts, and data — including scope of exposure</li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-400 mt-1"></i> Indicators of Compromise (IOCs) identified — hashes, IPs, domains</li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-400 mt-1"></i> Actions taken at each IR lifecycle stage and by whom</li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-400 mt-1"></i> Root cause analysis and lessons learned / remediation recommendations</li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-400 mt-1"></i> Regulatory notification requirements met (if PII/PHI involved)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 9: THREAT INTELLIGENCE ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec9" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-magnifying-glass-chart text-cyan-400"></i> 9. Threat Intelligence (CTI)</h2>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🔄 The CTI Lifecycle</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-4">Cyber Threat Intelligence follows a continuous, cyclical process ensuring intelligence remains relevant and actionable:</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-6 gap-2">
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40 text-center"><div class="badge badge-blue mb-2 mx-auto">1. Planning</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Define intelligence requirements based on stakeholder needs.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40 text-center"><div class="badge badge-amber mb-2 mx-auto">2. Collection</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Gather raw data from feeds, OSINT, internal logs.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40 text-center"><div class="badge badge-green mb-2 mx-auto">3. Processing</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Normalize, deduplicate, and structure raw data.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40 text-center"><div class="badge badge-red mb-2 mx-auto">4. Analysis</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Convert data into actionable assessments.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40 text-center"><div class="badge" style="background:rgba(168,85,247,.12);color:#c084fc;border:1px solid rgba(168,85,247,.3)">5. Dissemination</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Deliver intel to the right consumers in usable form.</p></div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40 text-center"><div class="badge badge-blue mb-2 mx-auto">6. Feedback</div><p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Refine future cycles based on consumer feedback.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🧩 Frameworks &amp; Methodologies</h3>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-pyramid"></i> Pyramid of Pain (PoP)</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A model ranking IOC types by how much "pain" it causes an attacker when defenders detect/block them. From bottom (easy for attackers to change) to top (hardest): Hash Values → IP Addresses → Domain Names → Network/Host Artifacts → Tools → Tactics, Techniques &amp; Procedures (TTPs). Detecting TTPs forces an attacker to fundamentally change their methodology — the ultimate goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-red mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-table-cells-large"></i> MITRE ATT&amp;CK Matrix</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A globally accessible knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques based on real-world observations, organized into a matrix of Tactics (the "why" — e.g. Initial Access, Persistence) and Techniques (the "how" — e.g. Phishing, Valid Accounts). Used to map detections, identify coverage gaps, and standardize threat reporting language across the industry.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-link"></i> Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A 7-stage linear model describing the typical progression of an intrusion: Reconnaissance → Weaponization → Delivery → Exploitation → Installation → Command &amp; Control → Actions on Objectives. Breaking the chain at any stage prevents the attack from completing — earlier disruption is always preferable.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-green mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-nodes"></i> Unified Cyber Kill Chain</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">An expanded model combining the Lockheed Martin Kill Chain with MITRE ATT&amp;CK concepts into 18 phases across three overarching stages — Initial Foothold, Network Propagation, and Action on Objectives — better reflecting the iterative, non-linear nature of modern attacks (e.g. repeated pivoting and privilege escalation cycles).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🧰 Interactive Multi-Tool Resource Grid</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-4">Technical profiles of the core CTI investigation tools used during the internship, including their role in the analyst workflow:</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">

      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-envelope-open-text text-blue-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">PhishTool</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-3">Email Forensics</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">PhishTool is a phishing email analysis platform that automatically parses email headers, extracts URLs and attachments, and enriches indicators against threat intel sources. Its role in investigation is to drastically speed up triage of user-reported phishing — an analyst can determine sender authenticity (SPF/DKIM/DMARC results), defang and extract IOCs, and classify the email's threat level within minutes rather than manually parsing raw headers.</p>
        <div class="placeholder-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-image text-2xl"></i><span class="text-xs text-center">[SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER: Tool Interface Analysis]</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-shield-virus text-cyan-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">Cisco Talos Intelligence</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-green mb-3">Reputation Lookup</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">Cisco Talos is one of the largest commercial threat intelligence teams, providing a free web portal for checking IP/domain/file reputation, viewing spam and malware trends, and accessing detailed vulnerability research. Analysts use Talos to quickly check whether a suspicious sending IP has a history of spam or malicious activity, providing fast supporting evidence during email and network investigations.</p>
        <div class="placeholder-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-image text-2xl"></i><span class="text-xs text-center">[SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER: Tool Interface Analysis]</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-link-slash text-amber-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">URLScan.io</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-3">Sandboxed URL Analysis</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">URLScan.io performs an automated sandboxed visit to a submitted URL, capturing a screenshot, the full DOM, all network requests, and any redirect chains — without exposing the analyst's own machine to risk. Its role in investigation is to safely reveal what a suspicious link actually does (e.g. a credential-harvesting login page mimic) before an analyst decides whether to block it organization-wide.</p>
        <div class="placeholder-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-image text-2xl"></i><span class="text-xs text-center">[SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER: Tool Interface Analysis]</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-database text-red-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">Abuse.ch</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-red mb-3">URLhaus · MalwareBazaar · SSL Blacklist</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">Abuse.ch operates a suite of free community-driven threat feeds: <strong>URLhaus</strong> tracks malware distribution URLs, <strong>MalwareBazaar</strong> is a repository of malware samples searchable by hash, and <strong>SSL Blacklist</strong> tracks malicious SSL certificates used by C2 infrastructure. Analysts use these feeds both to enrich IOCs during investigation and to feed automated blocklists directly into firewalls and SIEM correlation rules.</p>
        <div class="placeholder-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-image text-2xl"></i><span class="text-xs text-center">[SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER: Tool Interface Analysis]</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-virus text-emerald-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">VirusTotal</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-green mb-3">Multi-Engine Scanning</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">VirusTotal aggregates results from 70+ antivirus engines and URL/domain blocklisting services into a single lookup for files, hashes, URLs, IPs, and domains. It is the de facto first stop for an analyst investigating any suspicious artifact — a quick hash lookup (MD5/SHA-256) immediately shows whether a file is known-malicious, and the "Relations" tab reveals related infrastructure used by the same campaign.</p>
        <div class="placeholder-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-image text-2xl"></i><span class="text-xs text-center">[SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER: Tool Interface Analysis]</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-paw text-orange-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">ThreatFox</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-3">IOC Sharing Platform</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">Also operated by Abuse.ch, ThreatFox is a platform for sharing Indicators of Compromise (IOCs) associated with specific malware families, mapped to MITRE ATT&amp;CK where possible. Analysts search ThreatFox to determine whether an observed IOC (an IP, domain, or hash) has been previously attributed to a known malware family — providing immediate context on the likely nature and severity of an incident.</p>
        <div class="placeholder-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-image text-2xl"></i><span class="text-xs text-center">[SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER: Tool Interface Analysis]</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card p-5 sm:col-span-2 lg:col-span-1">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-server text-purple-400"></i><span class="font-bold text-slate-100">Feodo Tracker</span></div>
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-3">Botnet C2 Tracking</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">Feodo Tracker (also from Abuse.ch) specifically tracks Command &amp; Control (C2) infrastructure associated with banking trojan and botnet malware families such as Emotet, Dridex, and TrickBot. Its role in investigation is to provide a continuously updated IP blocklist — if an internal host is observed communicating with an IP on the Feodo Tracker list, this is high-confidence evidence of an active botnet infection requiring immediate containment.</p>
        <div class="placeholder-box"><i class="fa-solid fa-image text-2xl"></i><span class="text-xs text-center">[SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER: Tool Interface Analysis]</span></div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6 mb-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🔬 Supporting Concepts</h3>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-amber w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-shuffle"></i> Fuzzing</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">An automated testing technique that feeds malformed, unexpected, or random data into a program's inputs to discover crashes, memory leaks, or vulnerabilities that could be exploited — primarily a tool of vulnerability research, but its findings often inform detection rule creation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-green w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-hashtag"></i> Hashing — MD5 &amp; SHA-256</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Cryptographic hash functions produce a fixed-length "fingerprint" of a file. MD5 (128-bit) is fast but cryptographically broken for security purposes — still used for quick lookups. SHA-256 (256-bit) is the current standard for integrity validation and malware identification due to its collision resistance. Analysts hash suspicious files and compare against VirusTotal/MalwareBazaar.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-blue w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-rotate"></i> OPSWAT</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">OPSWAT provides multi-scanning technology (MetaDefender) that scans files with dozens of antivirus engines simultaneously, plus Deep CDR (Content Disarm and Reconstruction) which sanitizes documents by removing potentially malicious active content (macros, embedded objects) while preserving usability — commonly deployed at file upload/email gateways.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🧪 Simulation &amp; Emulation — Atomic Red Team</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">Defensive validation is the practice of testing whether your detection stack actually catches known attack techniques — rather than assuming it does. <strong class="text-emerald-400">Atomic Red Team</strong> is an open-source library of small, focused "atomic tests," each mapped directly to a specific MITRE ATT&amp;CK technique (e.g. T1003 — OS Credential Dumping). A Tier 3 analyst or detection engineer executes these atomic tests in a controlled environment and verifies that the SIEM/EDR generates the expected alert — closing the loop between threat intelligence (knowing what attackers do) and operational readiness (proving you can detect it). This is a cornerstone of continuous detection engineering and purple teaming exercises.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 10: EVENT CORRELATION ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec10" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-link text-blue-400"></i> 10. Event Correlation</h2>
  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">⚙️ Mechanics of Correlation</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-4">Event correlation is the process by which SIEM engines cross-reference disparate logs to identify relationships that reveal multi-stage attacks invisible in any single log source. Correlation operates across three primary dimensions:</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-clock"></i> Temporal Correlation</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Links events based on their timing relative to one another — e.g. a failed VPN login followed by a successful login from the same account within a tight time window, or a file download immediately followed by process execution of that same file.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-diagram-project"></i> Logical Correlation</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Links events based on cause-and-effect relationships defined by rule logic — e.g. "IF a port scan is detected FROM an external IP, AND THEN a connection to an internal database occurs FROM that same IP, raise a high-severity alert."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-green mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-id-card"></i> Identity Correlation</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Links events across different systems by a common identity (username, IP address, device ID, session token) — e.g. tracking a single compromised user account's activity across email, VPN, file server, and cloud application logs to build a full picture of attacker movement.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mt-4">By combining these dimensions, a SIEM can detect an attack pattern like: <em class="text-slate-400">"User A logs in from an unusual country (identity + temporal) → 10 minutes later, that same user account attempts to access 50 file shares it has never touched before (logical) → followed by a large data transfer to an external IP (temporal)."</em> Individually, none of these three events might trigger a high-severity alert — but correlated together, they represent a textbook data exfiltration pattern.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 11: USE CASES ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec11" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-bullseye text-emerald-400"></i> 11. Use Cases</h2>
  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🎯 Designing Real-World Detection Logic</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-4">A "use case" in SOC terminology is a documented detection scenario — a specific threat behavior, the data sources needed to detect it, the logic/query that identifies it, and the response actions it should trigger. Designing effective use cases requires balancing detection coverage against false positive rates. Three common examples:</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-red mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-key"></i> Brute Force Detection</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-2"><strong>Logic:</strong> Alert when a single source IP or user account generates N failed authentication attempts within a defined time window (e.g. 10 failures in 5 minutes), especially if followed by a successful login.</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500"><strong>Data sources:</strong> Windows Security logs (4625/4624), VPN logs, application auth logs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-plane"></i> Impossible Travel</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-2"><strong>Logic:</strong> Alert when the same user account authenticates successfully from two geographically distant locations within a time period that would make physical travel impossible (e.g. London then Tokyo within 1 hour).</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500"><strong>Data sources:</strong> Identity provider logs (Entra ID, Okta) with geolocation enrichment on source IP.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-red mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-lock"></i> Ransomware Behavior</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-2"><strong>Logic:</strong> Alert on a high rate of file modification/rename/encryption events on a single host within a short window — particularly mass file renames to unusual extensions, plus deletion of Volume Shadow Copies (vssadmin).</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500"><strong>Data sources:</strong> EDR file system telemetry, Sysmon Event ID 11 (file create), process execution logs.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 12: DETECTION RULES ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec12" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-code text-purple-400"></i> 12. Detection Rules</h2>
  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">👨‍💻 The Role of the Detection Engineer</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-4">A Detection Engineer is responsible for the full lifecycle of a detection rule: creating it based on threat intelligence or use case requirements, tuning it to minimize false positives without missing true positives, and maintaining it as attacker techniques evolve (rules that worked last year may be silently bypassed by new evasion methods). This is an ongoing arms race — detection engineering is never "done."</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mt-2">
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-magnifying-glass"></i> Sigma</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A generic, open signature format for SIEM detections written in YAML. Sigma rules are "write once, convert anywhere" — a single Sigma rule can be translated into native query syntax for Splunk SPL, QRadar AQL, Elastic, and others, making it the lingua franca for sharing detection logic across the industry.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-file-code"></i> YARA</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A pattern-matching tool used primarily for identifying and classifying malware samples based on textual or binary patterns within files. Detection engineers write YARA rules to scan file repositories or endpoint memory for signatures of known malware families, even when the file hash itself has changed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
        <div class="badge badge-green mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-network-wired"></i> Snort</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">An open-source Network Intrusion Detection/Prevention System (NIDS/NIPS) rule language used to inspect network traffic in real time. Snort rules match on packet headers and payload content to detect exploitation attempts, C2 beaconing, and policy violations directly on the wire.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 13: ANALYTICAL MATRIX ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec13" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-table-cells text-amber-400"></i> 13. The Analytical Matrix (Confusion Matrix)</h2>
  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📊 Classifying Alert Outcomes</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-4">Every alert outcome can be classified along two axes — whether the alert fired and whether a real threat was actually present:</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div class="matrix-cell" style="background:rgba(16,185,129,.06);border-color:rgba(16,185,129,.3)">
        <div class="badge badge-green mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check"></i> True Positive (TP)</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">An alert correctly identified a real malicious activity. This is the ideal detection outcome — the system worked as intended and a genuine threat was caught.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.06);border-color:rgba(239,68,68,.3)">
        <div class="badge badge-red mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-exclamation"></i> False Positive (FP)</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">An alert fired, but the activity was actually benign. Excessive FPs cause alert fatigue and erode analyst trust in the detection — a primary target for rule tuning.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,.06);border-color:rgba(59,130,246,.3)">
        <div class="badge badge-blue mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-minus"></i> True Negative (TN)</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">No alert fired, and indeed no malicious activity occurred. The vast majority of all activity in any environment falls into this category — the "quiet baseline."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="matrix-cell" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,.08);border-color:rgba(245,158,11,.35)">
        <div class="badge badge-amber mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> False Negative (FN)</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">No alert fired, but malicious activity DID occur — the most dangerous outcome. Represents a detection gap; a real attack went unnoticed. Minimizing FNs is the primary goal of threat hunting (Section 14).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 matrix-cell bg-slate-800/40">
      <div class="badge" style="background:rgba(168,85,247,.12);color:#c084fc;border:1px solid rgba(168,85,247,.3)">🟣 Benign Positive / Benign Negative</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-2"><strong>Benign Positive:</strong> An alert correctly fired on activity that is technically as-described but was authorized/expected — e.g. a vulnerability scan triggered an "exploit attempt" alert, but it was the organization's own scheduled pentest. The alert logic was correct, but no response action is needed beyond documentation.<br><br><strong>Benign Negative:</strong> No alert fired, and correctly so, for activity that superficially resembles a known pattern but is contextually normal — e.g. an admin's legitimate after-hours access during a scheduled maintenance window did not trigger the "off-hours access" rule due to a pre-approved exception list.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 14: THREAT HUNTING ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec14" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-binoculars text-emerald-400"></i> 14. Threat Hunting</h2>
  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">🔭 Core Philosophy</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mb-4">Threat Hunting is the proactive, hypothesis-driven exploration of an environment to uncover hidden threats that have bypassed existing automated security controls — it operates on the assumption that some attackers are already inside and simply haven't triggered an alert yet. This is fundamentally different from alert-driven SOC work (Sections 6-8), which is reactive by nature.</p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-2">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-amber w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-flask"></i> Hypothesis-Driven</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A hunt begins with a specific, testable hypothesis — often informed by threat intelligence about a new technique, an industry-wide campaign, or an observed gap in detection coverage. Example hypothesis: "If an APT group is using DNS tunneling for C2, we would see abnormally long or high-entropy DNS query strings to rarely-seen domains."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="badge badge-green w-fit"><i class="fa-solid fa-magnifying-glass-arrow-right"></i> Proactive Exploration</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">The hunter manually queries raw telemetry (not waiting for an alert) to test the hypothesis — using tools like SIEM search, EDR query languages, or notebook-based analysis. If evidence supporting the hypothesis is found, it becomes a confirmed incident; if not, the hypothesis is refined or a new detection rule is created to automate future checks.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300 mt-4">A successful threat hunt often results in two outcomes: the discovery of a previously undetected compromise (a "False Negative" caught proactively, per Section 13), and/or the creation of a brand-new detection rule (Section 12) so that the same pattern is automatically caught next time — directly feeding the continuous improvement loop that defines mature SOC operations.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 15: ESCALATION MATRIX ═══════════ -->
<section id="sec15" class="scroll-pt mb-14">
  <h2 class="section-title mb-4"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-up-right-dots text-red-400"></i> 15. Escalation Matrix</h2>
  <div class="section-card p-6">
    <h3 class="sub-title mt-0">📶 Tier-Based Operational Workflow</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-4">During a critical incident, an alert moves through a structured chain of escalation — each tier adding deeper expertise and broader authority until the appropriate stakeholders are engaged:</p>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <div class="flow-box flex-1 w-full text-left" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,.06);border-color:rgba(59,130,246,.3)">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="badge badge-blue">TIER 1</span><span class="font-bold">Triage</span></div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 font-normal mt-1">Initial alert review. Validates the alert is not an obvious false positive. If suspicious and beyond standard runbook resolution, escalates with full context (5 W's) to Tier 2.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-center"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-600"></i></div>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <div class="flow-box flex-1 w-full text-left" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,.06);border-color:rgba(245,158,11,.3)">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="badge badge-amber">TIER 2</span><span class="font-bold">Analysis</span></div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 font-normal mt-1">Deep investigation — correlates across multiple log sources, confirms scope and severity. If confirmed as a significant incident (e.g. active lateral movement, data exfiltration), escalates to Tier 3 with preliminary containment already initiated.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-center"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-600"></i></div>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <div class="flow-box flex-1 w-full text-left" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.06);border-color:rgba(239,68,68,.3)">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="badge badge-red">TIER 3</span><span class="font-bold">Handling</span></div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 font-normal mt-1">Advanced analysis, malware reverse engineering if needed, leads full containment/eradication execution per the IR lifecycle. Determines if the incident meets the threshold for formal CSIRT activation and executive notification.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-center"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-600"></i></div>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
